{
  "title": "What time is it?",
  "title_target": "Zer ordu da?",
  "goal": "Tell the time, name the days, months, and dates, and ask when something starts and ends.",
  "bekal": [
    "answer *zer ordu da?* correctly, including half past and quarter past/to",
    "name the seven days, twelve months, and four seasons",
    "use *-etan* (\"at … o'clock\") and *-tik … arte* (\"from … to …\")"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Elkarrizketa — Klaseko ordutegia",
      "intro": "Outside the euskaltegi door, Budi is in a panic, sure he's late.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Barkatu, Ainhoa, zer ordu da?",
          "id": "Sorry, Ainhoa, what time is it?",
          "literal": "sorry, Ainhoa, what hour is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ainhoa:",
          "text": "Bederatziak eta laurden dira.",
          "id": "It's a quarter past nine.",
          "literal": "nine-the(pl) and quarter are."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Jo! Klasea bederatzietan hasten da!",
          "id": "Oh no! Class starts at nine!",
          "literal": "oh! class-the nine(pl)-at starting is!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ainhoa:",
          "text": "Lasai, gaur hamarretan hasten gara.",
          "id": "Relax, today we start at ten.",
          "literal": "calm, today ten(pl)-at starting are-we."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "A, ondo. Eta noiz amaitzen da?",
          "id": "Oh, good. And when does it finish?",
          "literal": "ah, good. and when finishing is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ainhoa:",
          "text": "Ordu bata eta erdietan.",
          "id": "At half past one.",
          "literal": "hour one-the and half(pl)-at."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Astelehenetan ere ordu berean?",
          "id": "Same time on Mondays too?",
          "literal": "Monday(pl)-on also hour same-at?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ainhoa:",
          "text": "Ez, astelehenetan arratsaldez izaten dugu, lauretatik seiak arte.",
          "id": "No, on Mondays we have it in the afternoon, from four to six.",
          "literal": "no, Monday(pl)-on afternoon-by having we-have-it, four(pl)-from six(pl) until."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Eta ostiraletan?",
          "id": "And on Fridays?",
          "literal": "and Friday(pl)-on?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ainhoa:",
          "text": "Ostiraletan ez dago klaserik. Asteburua libre!",
          "id": "There's no class on Fridays. The weekend's free!",
          "literal": "Friday(pl)-on not is(located) class-any. weekend-the free!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bikain. Larunbatean zer egiten duzu?",
          "id": "Great. What do you do on Saturdays?",
          "literal": "excellent. Saturday-on what doing you-have-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ainhoa:",
          "text": "Normalean mendira joaten naiz. Etorri nahi duzu?",
          "id": "I usually go to the mountains. Want to come?",
          "literal": "usually mountain-to going am. come want you-have-it?"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Hours are plural, except one o'clock.** *Bederatziak dira* (it's nine o'clock — literally \"the nines are\"), *hirurak dira*, *hamarrak dira*. Only one o'clock is singular: *ordu bata da*. The logic is simple: Basque counts the hours already struck, and \"one\" can't be plural.",
        "**At what time: the ending -etan.** *Bederatzi**etan*** (at nine), *hamar**retan*** (at ten), *ordu bat**ean*** (at one). It's the place ending *-etan* you know from Unit 3, borrowed for time — just as English uses \"at\" for both: \"at home\", \"at nine\".",
        "**Astelehenetan = every Monday.** A day name made plural with *-etan* means a repeated habit: *astelehenetan* (on Mondays), *larunbatetan* (on Saturdays). For one particular Monday, use the singular: *astelehenean* (on Monday).",
        "**Hasi and amaitu take the auxiliary without the hat.** *Klasea hasten **da*** (the class starts), not *du* — because nobody is \"starting\" anything here; the class starts by itself. Compare: *Nik klasea hasten **dut*** means \"I start the class\"."
      ]
    }
  ],
